The Foundling Pens: a municipal menagerie of orphaned hatchlings nobody claimed. Mother Kestrel looks up from the rows.
mother_kestrel: So you're Maddox's new stray. He sends me one a season. I bury most of what he sends. Don't take it personal — the job buries most of everything.
mother_kestrel: I don't hand them out. Walk the rows. Let one come to you. The bond starts the moment something decides you're worth the risk. Most of them won't. They've got better instincts than you do. That's not an insult. It's the menagerie.
[You walk the rows. One Eros-fringe hatchling — hurt, small — limps to the bars. A second, healthier one watches from the back, wary.]
mother_kestrel: There. The little one with the bad leg. It picked you. Or it picked the first warm thing that didn't flinch. Out here those are the same choice. Now — how do you want to do this. This is the part that tells the world what kind of person showed up today.
- Offer my hand. Let it come the rest of the way.
	[signal arg="choice:offer_hand"]
	mother_kestrel: Gentle hands. The gods had those once. Look how that ended for them. It's bonded warm. That's rare and it's a liability — the ones you love don't end up on the table. Mostly. Off you go. Maddox'll want to see you didn't die.
- Bind it fast — wound-and-cinch. It's already hurt; I'll be quick.
	[signal arg="choice:bind_fast"]
	mother_kestrel: Works, don't it. Faster. Costs the beast something. Costs you a little more. You'll stop noticing — that's the trouble. It's yours. It's cold. Feed it by hand a week if you want it to stop watching the door.
- Let the healthy one and the runt settle it first. I'll take the survivor.
	[signal arg="choice:let_them_fight"]
	mother_kestrel: That's one way. The strong one's yours now and it learned its first lesson off the body of its kin. It'll be good. It'll also never quite trust the quiet. The runt goes in the Yard. Goodwife Penne logs them all. I'll walk it over myself. Someone decent should.
